Can anyone offer some insight on how to incorporate spatial covariates into the ssDF? I have two separate sites that I want to include in the same model, and I want to incorporate their location as a covariate. I have location included as a trap covariate, but can't figure out how to add a covariate to the ssDF. Any help would be appreciated.

Thanks!

On Wednesday, December 6, 2017 at 6:28:30 PM UTC-8, Chris Sutherland wrote:

> Terrific!
> Please let us know (offlist maybe) if there are things you feel could be improved to help along the learning curve!
>
> Chris

> On Dec 6, 2017 9:24 PM, "Nina Morrell" <morrel...@gmail.com> wrote:

> > Hi all,
> >
> > Some rearranging of my data inputs and I've resolved this issue - adding spatial covariates now (thankfully) improves model fit instead of drastically decreasing it. Possibly an obvious fix, but after seeing your responses I realized I should have the spatial covariates in the ssDF, not an rsfDF since I'm not using telemetry data. The resolution of the rsfDF also did not match the ssDF - now the spatial covariates use the same pixel coordinates as the state space. Still getting the hang of the oSCR set up but everything seems to be working great now!
> >
> > Thanks for the helpful responses!
> >
> > Nina
> >
> > On Monday, December 4, 2017 at 6:00:45 PM UTC-8, Daniel Linden wrote:
> > > Hi Nina, are you integrating telemetry data?  Given your reference to the rsfDF, it sounds like you are.  And such a jump in AIC looks reasonable depending on how many telemetry points there are.
> > >
> > > In which case, you cannot compare models with and without the telemetry data in terms of AIC.
> > >
> > > On Mon, Dec 4, 2017 at 8:56 PM, Jeffrey Royle <jar...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > hi Nina,
> > > >  I think any spatial covariate should not increase the AIC by more than 2 units!  I'd be curious to see the output of those models..... what is the spatial covariate? Show a summary of that please.
> > > > regards
> > > > andy
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > On Mon, Dec 4, 2017 at 8:52 PM, Nina Morrell <morrel...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > Hi Chris,
> > > > >
> > > > > Amazing, thank you so much for your prompt suggestions. I adapted my script to match your changes - the biggest change was defining the state space data frame using the make.ssDF function (as opposed to importing pixel coordinates) and trimming the state space as an argument to the model fitting function. Even when keeping the 250m resolution the models run much faster, but I also reduced the resolution to 1km as Andy suggested which greatly improved run time as well. As a result I can run models the full dataset with spatial covariates in under 3 hours, which is orders of magnitude faster. So, it worked! Also good to know about the binomial models.
> > > > >
> > > > > Just wondering, I noticed that there is a huge jump in the AIC values (ie. >200) when the models incorporate any of the spatial covariates in the rsfDF. Is this really suggesting that the spatial covariates are not contributing to space use by this species, as compared to the null model and models with just behavioral variables like sex? Or does the addition of the RSF make it so that the models are not really comparable by AIC to models without spatial covariates? Hope that question is clear, but let me know if I can clarify.
> > > > >
> > > > > Again thanks for your help, much appreciated!
> > > > >
> > > > > Nina
